  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 358 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA weak upper level disturbance is moving from west to east across\nNorth Central MT this morning, producing a few thunderstorms along\nthe divide and scattered showers over the Plains of North Central\nMT. That trend will continue through the morning hours, before the\nprecipitation starts to diminish this afternoon. Generally quiet\nconditions expected tonight through Sunday morning, before the\nnext upper level low arrives by Sunday evening. The forecast\nmodels have been decreasing precipitation amounts with this\nsystem, and also pulling the precip a bit further north. This\nsystem exits the region on Monday, with showers decreasing.\n\nFor Tuesday through Friday of next week, northwesterly flow aloft\ndevelop by Wednesday, as a another upper level disturbance moves\nsouthward through the area, producing some scattered showers. This\ndisturbance quickly exits the area by Thursday as an upper level\nridge tries to rebuild over the Northern Rockies by Friday. This\nwill result in warmer air moving back into the CWA by next Friday.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern is how much precipitation falls Sun into Mon and\nhow low do the snow levels get. Overall the forecast models\ngenerally have the max QPF of only around an inch over the\nNorthern Rockies. The probability of 1 inch of QPF in Glacier Park\nis only 60 percent, with less than a 20 percent over much of the\nnorthern Plains. Thus snow accumulations look mainly to be in the\nadvisory criteria, of 3 to 7 inches. Snow levels will be slow to\nfall as well, but could fall down to about 6000 feet or so by\nMonday morning. It is likely that a winter statement will be\nneeded for the Glacier Park area for this event.\n\nOtherwise, just expect much cooler temperatures over the CWA for\nthe next week or so. That being said, temperatures will be quite a\nbit cooler over North Central MT compared to Southwest MT, where\nafternoon temperatures over Southwest MT will still be mostly in\nthe upper 60s and lower 70s yet. Brusda",
